# Flutter/Dart security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' security rules to Flutter/Dart risks (OWASP Mobile Top 10) and the safe API for each.

## Secret and credential storage

- Store a token, session cookie, refresh token or other credential in
  `flutter_secure_storage` (Keychain on iOS, Android Keystore on Android; check
  the plugin's current backing implementation rather than assuming
  `EncryptedSharedPreferences`, which Google no longer recommends for new Android
  code), never in `shared_preferences`.
- `shared_preferences` stays fine for non-sensitive preferences (a theme choice,
  a "seen onboarding" flag).
- Never hard-code an API key, client secret or signing credential as a Dart string
  literal. `--dart-define` and a build-time `.env` file are compiled into the
  binary like a literal; they only keep the secret out of source control. A value
  that must stay off the device is fetched at runtime from a backend-issued
  short-lived token or a secrets manager over an authenticated call.

## Network calls and certificate pinning

- Use `https://` for every network call carrying user data, credentials or tokens.
- Certificate pinning (`HttpClient.badCertificateCallback` validated against a
  known pin, `http_certificate_pinning`, or platform-native pinning) is a
  deliberate project-level decision with a rotation plan for especially sensitive
  data (payment, health, authentication), not a default for every request.
- Never override `badCertificateCallback` (or an equivalent hook) to return
  `true` unconditionally, outside a short-lived local test.

## Platform channel and input validation

- Validate and sanitize any value crossing a `MethodChannel`/`EventChannel` in
  either direction before using it to index a collection, build a file path or
  construct a query.
- Encode structured data crossing a channel with the channel's codec (the
  standard codec or an explicit `MessageCodec`), not hand-rolled string
  concatenation/parsing.

## Local storage and logging

- Never write a secret, token or PII value to `debugPrint`/`print` or any logging
  sink that a release build ships enabled.
- A `WebView` rendering user-controlled or externally-fetched HTML must not run
  JavaScript on untrusted content unless it is sanitized first.

## Dependency hygiene

- Run `flutter pub outdated` and review the changelog/CVE history before a
  major-version bump; use `dart pub deps` to see what a new transitive dependency
  pulls in before adding it.
